{"line": "header", "transcript": "12-crash-before-checkpoint", "demonstrates": "The crash boundary on both sides of a checkpoint. A run makes one record durable, commits a checkpoint, makes a second record durable, and then dies before the second checkpoint. The next run resumes from the committed cursor, replays the second object, and changes no byte.", "gate": "a2-normative", "operation": "collect", "mode": "incremental", "expected_outcome": "failed", "expected_exit_code": 137, "notes": "The cursor deliberately lags durable state. Replay is idempotent through the portable exact-source key, so a lagging cursor costs work and never correctness, while an advanced cursor would silently lose an object forever."}
